The user wants me to improve the Boeing-747 model based on judge feedback.

Weakest feature according to the judges is `wing_geometry` (mean 4.88/10).

Target improvement: "clearly swept-back (~35-40 deg) tapering wings with visible upward dihedral, mounted at the lower mid-fuselages; then the 10 anchor: realistic planform: strong sweep, root-to-tip chord and thickness taper, dihedral, plausible span (~0.9x body length), wing roots faired into the belly."

Current implementation of `wing_geometry`:
```javascript
  const createWingHalf = (side) => {
    const half = new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.BoxGeometry(1, 0.4, 35), dark);
    half.position.set(side * 2, -1.2, side * 15);
    half.rotation.x = Math.PI / 180 * 3; // Dihedral
    half.rotation.z = -Math.PI / 180 * 30; // Sweep back
    half.rotation.y = side === 1 ? Math.PI / 2 : -Math.PI / 2; // Align span with Z axis
    wingGroup.add(half);
  };
```

Observations from `view-1.png`:
1. The wings are indeed very flat and don't have much taper or sweep.
2. They look like slabs attached to the side of the fuselage rather than integrated into the belly.
3. The dihedral is very small (3 degrees).
4. There is no tapering at all; it's just a long box.

The target is 10/10: "realistic planform: strong sweep, root-to-tip chord and thickness taper, dihedral, plausible span (~0.9x body length), wing roots faired into the belly."

Plan for improvement:
Instead of one `BoxGeometry` for each half-wing, I'll use a multi-part approach to simulate taper and some sort of root join. Since I can only use primitives, two boxes (root and tip) might be best. Or even three (inner, mid, outer).

